\begin{abstract}
The integration of Terrestrial and Non-Terrestrial Networks (TN-NTNs), which was introduced in 5G, is progressing toward a unified and seamless network of networks in Sixth-Generation (6G). This evolution leads to a significant increase in the volume of generated and communicated data, imposing technical and operational requirements accompanied by a higher cost and energy consumption. Efficiently managing the generation and transmission of data in these highly complex unified networks has become essential. In this article, we investigate the semantics-aware information handling problem within unified TN-NTNs, where data communication between the distant TN nodes is enabled via an NTN. To this end, an Internet of Things (IoT) monitoring system is employed, where status updates from a remote IoT device are communicated to a destination monitor via a constellation of Low Earth Orbit (LEO) satellites. We leverage semantic metrics that capture the timeliness, relevance, and utility of information to provide the most informative data for timely and informed decision-making and eventually reduce the volume of transmitted and processed data. The outcome is significantly lower energy consumption, memory, control, and processing requirements (up to $73\%$ lower energy charging demands compared to the state-of-the-art), all without compromising the conveyed information.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Semantics-aware communication, LEO satellites, Query Version Age of Information (QVAoI), Energy harvesting.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Integrating Non-Terrestrial Networks (NTNs) with Terrestrial Networks (TNs) promises extended, seamless coverage, and ubiquitous connectivity on a global scale. This integration establishes a unified infrastructure for communication and utilization of information across various applications and services worldwide, including those on ships, airplanes, or in remote rural areas\cite{geraci2022integrating}, within a three-dimensional (3D) network architecture that encompasses terrestrial, aerial, and space layers\cite{ntontinether}. These applications include a wide range of use cases, including global asset monitoring, smart agriculture, smart cities, autonomous vehicles, environmental monitoring, industrial IoT, Public Protection and Disaster Relief  (PPDR), and healthcare in remote areas \cite{ntontin2025vision}. 

Nevertheless, as this integration progresses towards unified 6G 3D networks, the number of energy-consuming network elements and compute and storage requirements increases significantly due to the substantial growth in the volume of generated and transmitted data across the 3D network. Remote IoT devices, aerial nodes (e.g., Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs)), and space nodes are battery-powered devices with limited or no access to energy supplies, highlighting the need to optimize energy management under constrained resources to maintain reliable services. Furthermore, bandwidth and propagation limitations in NTNs may lead to congestion or delays, emphasizing the importance of resource allocation and the prioritization of critical communications, such as emergency services, during periods of peak demand. Efficient traffic management is essential to ensure timely communication between the TN and NTN layers without compromising the quality of service (QoS). Achieving reliable, high-speed, and low-latency communication of large volumes of information across numerous highly heterogeneous interconnected TN and NTN nodes requires energy-efficient and scalable information handling approaches. 


In this context, the \emph{semantics-aware communication} paradigm employs goal-oriented approaches to address the generation and transmission of the most informative data, where the innate and task-driven attributes of information are considered to achieve the goal of communication. Semantic approaches have been shown to reduce the volume of generated, exchanged, and processed data while ensuring efficient resource allocation within the network \cite{kountouris2021semantics,uysal2022semantic}. This methodology has been effectively applied in TN IoT networks and status update systems \cite{abd2019role,yates2021age}. However, it requires significantly more attention in unified TNs and NTNs, where the communication of uninformative data and the wastage of resources can result in higher costs. This stems from the fact that additional generation and transmission of uninformative data leads to power waste and consequently to the lifetime decrease of the NTN platforms, thus overlooking their SWaP (Size, Weight, and Power) limitations. 

Recent studies have initiated the incorporation of semantics in NTN networks \cite{ngo2024timeliness,liao2024information}. However, their primary focus has been limited to one aspect of semantics, i.e., timeliness. In contrast, other aspects, such as the relevance and utility of information, which reflect the true significance of data, have been largely overlooked. Moreover, most of these studies focus on UAVs or single-satellite setups, disregarding the potential of networked satellite configurations and other platforms, such as high-altitude platforms (HAPs). By addressing these gaps and effectively integrating semantics-aware approaches within TN-NTNs, we can facilitate significant advancements for next-generation 6G networks.

To this end, this article proposes integrating semantics-aware information handling into a unified NTN-TN framework, which includes a network of interconnected LEO satellites. This approach employs semantic metrics that capture not only the timeliness of the information but also its relevance and utility. Furthermore, an optimal information handling policy is investigated for status updates from a remote, energy-limited IoT device to monitoring nodes, where the LEO satellites can function as relaying devices or end users. The results indicate that, compared to the state-of-the-art (SoA) information handling schemes, the incorporation of content-based semantics leads to a significant reduction in the number of transmissions from the device, thereby decreasing dissemination in the LEO network. This reduction leads to lower energy consumption and cost savings by reducing the requirements for transmissions, computations, memory, and control operations. 

The rest of this article is organized as follows.
Section \ref{Sec_MainTechChallenges} presents the main technical challenges in 6G unified TN-NTNs. In Section \ref{Sec_SemanticComm}, we introduce and explain the semantics-aware communication approach. The optimal semantics-aware policy for information handling in a unified TN-NTN setup is determined in Section \ref{Sec_SemanticSystem} and evaluated in Section \ref{Sec_Results}. Finally, we conclude the article in Section \ref{Sec_Conclusion} by outlining future directions.

\section{Main Technical Challenges}

\label{Sec_MainTechChallenges}

Efficient information handling and data management are crucial in unified TN-NTNs due to inherent constraints such as high latency, mobility, interference, and energy limitations. As next-generation networks generate vast amounts of data, optimizing resource utilization becomes essential for managing this data and reducing energy consumption, ensuring reliable and continuous services across the 3D network. The main technical challenges from the perspective of data management are as follows. 

\begin{itemize}
\item{\textbf{Latency Management:}} Unified TN-NTNs may experience significant latency, particularly in satellite communications, due to the vast distances that signals must travel. For instance, GEO satellites, operating at an altitude of $35786$ km from Earth's surface, introduce approximately $250$ ms two-way delay, which can severely impact real-time or time-critical applications such as voice calls, remote control, and monitoring systems. The problem is exacerbated in multi-hop satellite networks or deep-space communications, making the timeliness and relevance of information critical in the unified TN-NTNs.

\item{\textbf{Energy Consumption and Costs:}} Signals in unified TN-NTNs suffer significant path loss due to long-distance propagation and atmospheric attenuation. Higher transmission power is required to mitigate this issue, leading to excessive energy consumption, critical for power-limited IoT devices and platforms such as satellites and UAVs. Furthermore, UAVs and HAPs have limited energy resources and continuous high-volume data transmissions rapidly deplete their batteries. This necessitates frequent landings for battery replacement or recharging, which increases downtime and operational costs. Minimizing data transmissions is crucial to conserve battery life, reduce maintenance frequency, enable green and sustainable communication, and improve the overall lifecycle. In this way, the operating expenses (OPEX) and, consequently, the total cost of ownership (TCO) can be reduced.

\item{\textbf{Handover and Service Continuity:}} NTN nodes, such as LEO satellites, UAVs, and HAPS, move rapidly relative to TN nodes, requiring frequent handovers and routing to maintain continuous services. The frequent exchange of information and control signaling can strain the network, demanding additional resources. Reducing unnecessary data transmissions is crucial to alleviating the burden on handover mechanisms and ensuring seamless connectivity.

\item{\textbf{Spectrum Scarcity and Interference:}} TN-NTNs operate within a highly congested spectrum, where coexisting transmissions result in interference and degraded signal quality. Furthermore, the rapid movement of NTN nodes introduces Doppler shifts, which affect OFDM-based communication systems and cause signal distortions. Excessive data transmissions exacerbate interference and reduce spectral efficiency. Therefore, minimizing redundant transmissions is essential for mitigating interference and optimizing spectrum utilization.

\item{\textbf{Massive and Diverse Data Traffic:}}
TN-NTNs must manage a wide range of diverse data types—such as real-time video, IoT telemetry, emergency alerts, and bulk downloads—each with distinct QoS requirements. Without effective management, uninformative data can congest the network, degrading QoS for critical applications, including PPDR and telemedicine.

\item{\textbf{Caching and Real-Time Processing Capabilities:}}
Satellites, UAVs, and edge IoT devices have restricted memory, storage, and processing capabilities, making it challenging to manage large-scale data transmissions in real time. Overloading these limited resources with stale or uninformative data leads to processing delays and ineffective services.

\end{itemize}

\section{Semantics-Aware Communication}
\label{Sec_SemanticComm}
Addressing the aforementioned technical challenges necessitates a careful \textit{focus on network and information management approaches}, considering the \textit{context and content of information within the entire chain of information handling process from the information generation to its utilization}. The semantics-aware methodology provides such solutions by enabling goal-oriented configurations and optimizing the relevant semantics of information within the 3D network. Specifically, it targets to significantly reduce or even eliminate the communication of stale and uninformative data within the network while satisfying QoS requirements. Consequently, significant energy savings and substantial reductions in latency and data congestion in the unified network can be achieved. In this section, we elaborate on the semantic attributes of information and metrics. Then, we describe a semantics-aware status update setup as a core configuration for data communication in unified TN-NTNs.

\begin{figure*}[tb!]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=4.8in]{StatusUpdateSystem.eps}
    \caption{An end-to-end status update system equipped with a semantic agent that considers the generation, transmission, and utilization of information.}
	\label{fig_StatusUpdateSys}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Semantic Attributes}
The incorporation of semantics of information into communication networks has demonstrated substantial benefits, beginning with the introduction of the Age of Information (AoI)\cite{kaul2012real}, which captures the timeliness of information within the network, and extending to other attributes, such as relevance and utility. The key semantic attributes are explained below.

\begin{itemize}
\item{\emph{Freshness or timeliness of information:}} AoI, going beyond the widely used metrics of delay and throughput, effectively represents the staleness of data in the network, reduces unnecessary outdated data exchanges, and frees up resources for more efficient and optimized information handling. This is achieved through a holistic approach encompassing the entire chain, from data generation to reception. AoI, which captures freshness or timeliness of information, contributes to the communication of the freshest possible information in the network. 
\item{\emph{Relevance of information:}} When considering the sampling or generation of data at the source, not all generated data conveys the correct piece of information content-wise. For example, when the content of information remains unchanged over a period, generating or transmitting repetitive content to enhance freshness may be wasteful. This underscores another critical aspect of semantic metrics, i.e., relevance of information, which focuses on the communication of data that carries relevant content at the source.
\item{\emph{Utility or value of information:}} Another drawback of AoI is that it primarily considers the chain of information from its generation time to its reception, neglecting its utilization at the destination node upon receipt. For instance, if the receiver is unavailable to receive the data or is too busy to process the received data, maintaining the timeliness and relevance of the information becomes insufficient. This introduces the third semantic attribute of information, i.e., utility or value of information, at the destination. This attribute emphasizes the effective communication of information when it is useful and valuable for utilization. 
\end{itemize}

These three attributes – timeliness, relevance, and utility – represent the important dimensions of semantics-aware communication, as they collectively capture the timeliness and significance of information, encompassing data generation, transmission, and eventual utilization
within the network.

\subsection{Semantic Metrics}
 The aforementioned semantic attributes are quantified through metrics, such as AoI, Version AoI (VAoI)\cite{yates2021vage}, Query AoI (QAoI)\cite{chiariotti2022query}, and Query VAoI (QVAoI)\cite{delfani2024semantics}. Optimizing these metrics in communication networks can significantly reduce resource usage while preserving the integrity of the information conveyed within the network. 

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{AoI} is a \emph{timeliness} metric that quantifies the time elapsed since the generation of the last successfully received data at the destination node. 
    \item \textbf{VAoI} is a semantic metric that jointly quantifies the \emph{timeliness} and \emph{relevance} of information by measuring the number of versions by which the receiver lags behind the source. A version of information is defined as any marked change in the content of information at the source. This metric has been analyzed and optimized in interconnected gossip networks\cite{buyukates2022version,DelfaniVage}.
    \item \textbf{QAoI} is another performance metric that represents both the \emph{timeliness} and \emph{utility} of information. QAoI considers the AoI only when there is a request from the destination node, i.e., only during query instances when the information is assumed to be useful to the receiver. 
    \item \textbf{QVAoI}, as an extension of both VAoI and QAoI, takes into account both content changes at the source and queries from the destination. This metric represents updates that are both \emph{relevant} and \emph{valuable}, alongside the \emph{freshest} ones. QVAoI is defined as the number of versions by which the receiver lags behind the source during query instances. This metric ensures that the system is not penalized in the absence of requests, and when the transmission of updates holds no value or utility for the receiver.
\end{itemize}


\begin{figure*}[tb!]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=4.8in]{SemanticMetrics.eps}
	\caption{Semantic metrics: AoI captures the timeliness of information, while VAoI and QAoI further encompass its relevance and utility. QVAoI integrates all three semantic attributes. The reception times of updates are denoted by $d_i$, the version generation instances by $g_i$, and the query instances by $q_i$.}
	\label{fig_SemanticMetrics}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Semantics-aware Scheduling}
In Fig. \ref{fig_StatusUpdateSys}, a basic end-to-end (E2E) status update system is depicted, which serves as a core configuration for data communication in IoT networks and real-time monitoring systems. In this system, the status of an information source is measured by a device acting as the source node and transmitted in the form of \emph{update packets} to a destination node through a forwarding network or a direct forwarding channel. These update packets are then processed or utilized at the destination node. In addition to the forward path, there may also be a backward channel from the destination node to the source node, responsible for transmitting the ACK feedback or the queries in the system. The queries in a status update system can result in a \emph{pull-based} status update, where the update packet can be requested (or pulled) from the source. In contrast, a \emph{push-based} setup forwards the updates to the destination regardless of queries. 

In this status update setup, the semantic metrics can be represented as a function of discrete time slots. An illustration is provided in Fig. \ref{fig_SemanticMetrics}, where data is generated in each time slot. We have assumed that the service time required for a generated update packet to be delivered to the destination node is equal to one time slot. Let us denote the reception instances by $d_i$, the version generation instances by $g_i$, and the query instances by $q_i$, where $i = 1, 2, \cdots$. At time $d_1$, the generated update packet from the previous time slot is delivered to the destination node, causing the AoI to drop to $1$ (indicating that one slot has elapsed since the generation of the received packet). The VAoI drops to $0$ because the information source and the destination node now contain the same status or version of information. At this point, both QAoI and QVAoI are zero since no query has been received from the destination node. Thereafter, the AoI increases linearly until the next reception instance ($d_2$). Meanwhile, five versions are generated at the information source (at instances $g_3$ to $g_7$), increasing the VAoI to $5$, as the destination node is now $5$ versions behind the information source. During this period, four successive queries (at $q_2$ to $q_5$) arrive from the destination node, where QAoI and QVAoI become equal to AoI and VAoI, respectively. The evolution of the semantic metrics continues in the same manner.

A semantics-aware agent is employed to control the information-handling chain, which encompasses the generation, transmission, and utilization of data, while accounting for the semantics of the information. Depending on the configuration, this agent can be positioned at the source, the destination, or both. In a push-based setup, the semantic agent is located at the source node, whereas in a pull-based setup, it may be positioned at either end. The semantic agent determines the scheduling policy according to which update packets are generated and exchanged within the system. This policy optimizes the semantic metrics, aiming to achieve an optimal information-handling scheme that delivers timely and informative data while ensuring the efficient utilization of network resources.


\section{Semantics-aware Unified TN-NTNs: \\ System Model and Optimization}
\label{Sec_SemanticSystem}

\begin{figure}[tb!]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=2.1in]{SatelliteNet.eps}
	\caption{Status updates via the LEO satellite network.}
	\label{fig_SatNet}
\end{figure}

\subsection{System Model}
Consider a remote IoT device located on a remote location on Earth, an autonomous ship, or even at a HAPS, that measures and sends status updates from an information source, which could be a physical process such as the state of an oil pipeline, temperature, humidity at a specific point, to a destination monitoring node, where no direct terrestrial link exists between the device and the monitor. This scenario can apply, e.g., to environmental monitoring of distant locations without terrestrial infrastructure, e.g., for polar ice melting monitoring, air quality, temperature, and pollution levels monitoring, or for a PPDR use case where terrestrial infrastructure has become unavailable. In this scenario, a connected Low Earth Orbit (LEO) satellite can receive the update packets from the device and relay them to the monitoring node, either through a direct forwarding channel or by passing the packet to a neighboring satellite in the same orbital plane (or possibly adjacent orbital planes) for eventual delivery to the monitor. The communication between LEO satellites occurs via Inter-Satellite Links (ISLs), e.g., high-capacity optical ISLs. This scenario is depicted in Fig. \ref{fig_SatNet}, which shows an IoT device and two possible destination monitoring nodes. The request from the monitoring node has also been incorporated into this model, where queries are transmitted to the device via the Connected Satellite (CS). 
In this system model, the remote device is assumed to be equipped with a rechargeable battery that is charged by harvesting energy from ambient sources such as solar or wind. This reflects the energy constraints on the device, which are common in many real-world scenarios. When the battery is depleted, the device is unable to transmit updates. Otherwise, it must decide on the action to be taken for transmissions, with decisions made sequentially over a slotted time axis. Each unit of the battery is assumed to be charged probabilistically at each time slot and is normalized to the energy consumed for transmission, i.e., each transmission consumes one energy unit of the battery. 

Each update packet from the information source is assigned a timestamp and a version number. New versions are generated at a specific rate or probability in each time slot. We assume a forward unreliable channel from the device to the receiver, through which data packets are transmitted, with a defined success probability. Additionally, an error-free backward channel from the receiver to the device is considered, enabling the transmission of queries and acknowledgments (ACKs) to confirm the successful reception of data packets. Upon receiving the ACK feedback, the device learns the most recent timestamp and version stored at the destination node and calculates the semantic metrics accordingly. The version generations, query arrivals, and energy arrivals can be modeled using stochastic Bernoulli processes.

We incorporate a semantic agent on the device to determine the optimal scheduling for the transmission of status updates to the destination monitor. The complete delivery of an update to a ground monitor involves a transmission from the device to the CS, multiple disseminations via ISLs within the LEO satellite network, and a final transmission from the last satellite to the ground monitor. In remote systems, this setup may involve $N$ inter-satellite disseminations (or hops) within the LEO network and a total of $N+2$ transmissions for delivering information from the IoT device to the monitoring node. Therefore, reducing the generation or transmission of a single update from the device directly decreases $N+2$ transmissions in the unified network, highlighting the significance of efficient information handling. By deploying a semantic agent on the device for optimal transmission scheduling, the system configuration shown in Fig. \ref{fig_StatusUpdateSys} is obtained, in which the IoT device acts as the source node, and the CS serves as the immediate destination node.

\subsection{Optimal Information Handling Policy}

The optimization of semantic metrics within this framework can be rigorously formulated as a Markov Decision Process (MDP). Specifically, the objective is to minimize the long-term expected value of each designated semantic metric through the derivation of an optimal update policy. At each discrete time slot, this optimal policy dictates whether to transmit or withhold an update based on the current system state, characterized by the semantic metric’s instantaneous value, the device’s battery status, and the query issued by the CS. The MDP model explicitly incorporates statistical descriptions of underlying stochastic processes, including energy replenishment (device charging), query arrivals from the CS, and information source version generation. These processes are modeled as independent Bernoulli random variables, enabling a straightforward representation of their average occurrence rates or probabilities per time slot, typically expressed as percentages.


\emph{By optimizing semantic metrics in the system, an optimal scheduling policy is derived, which offers benefits from two perspectives: first, the freshest and most informative status updates are transmitted within the network, enabling precise monitoring and informed decision-making at the monitor, all while adhering to energy constraints; second, the number of transmissions from the device to the CS, and consequently the number of relayed packets among the satellites, is reduced by excluding stale and uninformative data from the network. This leads to a significant reduction in energy and cost consumption within the network}, as will be demonstrated in the next section.

\section{Evaluation Results}
\label{Sec_Results}
\subsection{Simulation Scenario}
In this section, we study the performance of the described system in delivering timely, relevant, and useful status updates to the network. This is captured by QVAoI at the CS, which directly determines the QVAoI at the satellite nodes and the destination monitor. The following policies adopted by the semantic agent are compared in terms of their ability to provide the optimal QVAoI in the network: 1) \textbf{Greedy policy}: transmit an update as soon as energy becomes available and the battery is not empty; 2) \textbf{AoI-aware}, 3) \textbf{VAoI-aware}, 4) \textbf{QAoI-aware}, and 5) \textbf{QVAoI-aware policies}: transmit the updates when optimizing the time average of AoI, VAoI, QAoI, and QVAoI, respectively.

We set the channel success probability to $80\%$ and the version generation rate to $25\%$. We evaluate the performance of the aforementioned policies under low and moderate energy charging and query rates.



\subsection{Policy Comparison}

\begin{figure*}[tb!]
	\centering
    \captionsetup[subfloat]{labelfont=small,textfont=small}
	\subfloat[Query rate $=10\%$]{\includegraphics[width=3.5in]{QVAoI_q0.1.eps}
	\label{fig_QVAoIq0.1}}
	\subfloat[Query rate $=30\%$]{\includegraphics[width=3.5in]{QVAoI_q0.3.eps}
	\label{fig_QVAoIq0.3}}
    \caption{QVAoI for low (a) and moderate (b) query rates: Utilizing additional semantic attributes results in fresher and more informative data within the network.}
\end{figure*}

In Figs. \ref{fig_QVAoIq0.1} and \ref{fig_QVAoIq0.3}, the performance of the greedy and semantics-aware policies are compared for low ($10\%$) and moderate ($30\%$) query rates under low  ($5\%$) and moderate ($20\%$) energy charging rates. As observed in all cases, adopting the optimal semantics-aware policies significantly improves the system's performance compared to the greedy policy, providing the freshest and most informative data from the information source to the CS. These figures also demonstrate the superior performance of VAoI-aware and QVAoI-aware policies compared to AoI-aware and QAoI-aware policies, respectively. By considering the relevance of information and its timeliness and utility, these policies offer a more effective scheduling scheme for transmitting updates when they are most relevant and informative while utilizing the same amount of energy resources.

Increasing the query rate from $10\%$ (Fig. \ref{fig_QVAoIq0.1}) to $30\%$ (Fig. \ref{fig_QVAoIq0.3}), the performance of the semantics-aware system may either improve or decline, depending on the energy charging rate, while consistently outperforming the greedy policy. For moderate to high energy charging rates, the system becomes less energy-constrained, allowing for the transmission of more updates. Consequently, the performance of the system becomes more dependent on the utility of the information, i.e., the query rate; therefore, an increase in query rate enhances the performance in terms of QVAoI. However, when the energy charging rate is low, the system becomes energy-restricted, and exerting more pressure by sending additional queries leads to a greater number of query instances with the same amount of updates, resulting in a deterioration of the QVAoI.

\begin{figure}[tb!]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=3.5in]{Actions.eps}
	\caption{Transmission regions for the greedy and semantics-aware policies: The semantics-aware transmission policy uses a threshold-based structure, initiating updates when the VAoI exceeds the threshold. The optimal policy sets higher thresholds at lower energy charging rates, especially with low battery levels. In contrast, the greedy policy ignores VAoI, transmitting only when the battery is non-empty.}
	\label{fig_Actions}
\end{figure}

To provide further insights into the policies, in Fig. \ref{fig_Actions}, the actions taken by the agent are displayed for both the greedy and semantics-aware policies. The QVAoI-aware policy has been selected to represent the semantics-aware policies, as it encapsulates all semantic attributes and demonstrates the best performance according to the evaluation results presented. Two cases of low and moderate charging rates have been considered for the optimal semantics-aware policy, where the query rate is $30\%$. 
The selected action (transmission or staying idle) is a function of the VAoI value at query instances (since no transmission occurs without a query) and the battery state, i.e., the number of charged and usable battery units. In the depicted figure, this leads to transmission regions where the action is to transmit an update; otherwise, the device remains idle and does not send an update. The greedy policy considers only the battery state, transmitting updates whenever the battery is not empty, resulting in the largest region, depicted in green. In contrast, the optimal semantics-aware policy takes into account the VAoI value at query instances (or QVAoI) and operates as follows: 

\begin{itemize}
    \item It never transmits updates when the VAoI is zero, i.e., when the device and the CS contain identical versions or content of information, as such updates are uninformative.
    \item It sets threshold levels on VAoI for each battery state to avoid transmitting updates when the versions at the CS are not significantly outdated. The optimal policy prioritizes sending updates at higher VAoI values, as transmitting at lower VAoI levels depletes the battery. This can result in long intervals without updates, during which the CS misses many newer versions, causing the QVAoI to grow to very high levels. As illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig_Actions}, the transmission region for the lower energy charging rate ($5\%$, in light orange) is smaller and characterized by higher VAoI thresholds compared to the scenario with a higher charging rate ($20\%$, in blue).
\end{itemize}

In addition to the aforementioned findings, the obtained results indicate that to maintain a specific level of QVAoI in the system, different policies require different energy charging rates. Fig. \ref{fig_NeededEnergy} illustrates the required energy charging rate for various policies to achieve an average QVAoI of $1.5$. This quantity is presented relative to (i.e., divided by) the energy charging rate of the greedy policy. As observed, semantics-aware policies significantly improve energy efficiency, with the QVAoI-aware policy requiring only $27\%$ of the energy charging rate (i.e., $73\%$ less) compared to the greedy policy. This substantial reduction in energy consumption is achieved by discarding stale, irrelevant, and non-utilizable information in the communication network. The reduced number of updates in the system also results in lower memory, storage, control, and processing requirements in the unified TN-NTN.

