\section{Conclusion and Future Directions}
\label{Sec_Conclusion}
This article investigated the semantics-aware information handling in unified TN-NTNs and introduced an optimal policy for managing status updates from energy-constrained IoT devices to monitoring nodes via a satellite network. By evaluating various semantic metrics under different charging and query rate scenarios, our study demonstrated that incorporating semantic-aware communication can significantly reduce the number of transmissions. This reduction leads to lower energy consumption and a substantial decrease in energy charging demands—up to $73\%$ lower than state-of-the-art (SoA) approaches. These findings underscore the transformative potential of semantics-aware information handling in contributing to developing sustainable and efficient next-generation TN-NTNs. Future research directions include extending this work to more complex scenarios, such as integrating the aerial domain for enhanced network adaptability and efficiency as well as investigating the impact of the semantic agent operation in the overall power consumption of the network.
