\bre[Improvements in Theorem \ref{t:theRealDeal}]
Theorem \ref{t:theRealDeal} below is stronger than Theorem \ref{t:simple} in that it considers arbitrary covers of $\Omega$, and bounds the high ($\gg k$) and low ($\lesssim k$) frequencies of the Galerkin error separately.
Two situations in which a more complicated cover is advantageous are the following. 1) There are two or more cavities that are dynamically separated, i.e., for which there is no billiard trajectory whose closure insects both cavities. 2) One has a priori information about the data and/or solution and hence can obtain good control on the right-hand side of~\eqref{e:simple}. Even when $\cavity=\emptyset$, such information combined with Theorem \ref{t:simple} allows one to define meshes with a priori improved accuracy in some regions, without the need to choose a small meshwidth everywhere.
