\section{Introduction}

With the continuous exponential growth of data, DLRM (Deep Learning Recommendation Models) are gradually becoming one of the core parts for major Internet platforms, which could enhance user experience and business value. In the design and optimization of DLRM, improving user engagement has become the key objective. %Improving user retention is the key object of DLRM, hence, identifying the user interests is crucial. %
To achieve this goal, an accurate identification of user interests is crucial. Through this way, the recommendation system could deliver the content that meets users need.
%Only by deeply understanding the evolution of user interests and combining it with dynamically adjusted recommendation strategies can the recommendation system ensure that it always delivers content that best meets user needs.

%Traditional recommender systems have progressed from shallow models to deep learning approaches for user interest identification. Collaborative filtering\citep{sarwar2001item} captures latent interests via user behavior but struggles with data sparsity and cold starts. 
Traditional methods have made some progress in identifying user interests.
Wide \& Deep\citep{cheng2016wide} combines linear and deep models to balance memorization and generalization to enhance the modeling of user interest. 
DeepFM\citep{guo2017deepfm} and DCN\citep{wang2017deep} extends this by modeling user interest interactions with incorporating factorization machines and cross networks respectively. DIN\citep{zhou2018deep} introduces attention mechanisms to adaptively weight user interests for fine-grained personalization. Above methods improve the accuracy and interpretability of user interest identification.

% However, real-world recommender systems often encompass multiple scenarios, with user behaviors spanning across different contexts, such as search, homepage recommendations, and short video recommendations in e-commerce platforms. Compared to single-scenario systems, integrating data from multiple scenarios can enrich user behavior samples and compensate for insufficient learning caused by limited data in certain scenarios. The fundamental goal of multi-scenario recommendation is to offer users a rich and diverse experience across different interaction points, while maintaining a consistent and coherent service quality. By seamlessly connecting different scenarios, platforms can guide users through various decision-making stages—browsing, searching, discovering, and purchasing—thereby improving user satisfaction and engagement. Nevertheless, the data distributions, user intents, and interaction patterns across these different scenarios may vary significantly. This can lead to a seesaw effect, where optimizing the recommendation performance for one scenario may degrade the performance in another. Therefore, multi-scenario modeling needs to not only focus on interest modeling within individual scenarios but also capture cross-scenario interest associations to more accurately meet user needs. This makes multi-scenario modeling an important challenge in recommendation system research.

