\section{Introduction}
% Molecule generation plays a crucial role in drug discovery, enabling the design of novel compounds with desired biological properties.
The Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry System (SMILES) \cite{weininger1988smiles} has been widely adopted as one of molecular representations for various molecular generation and prediction tasks \cite{segler2018generating,bjerrum2017molecular,irwin2022chemformer,bagal2021molgpt}.
Large language model (LLM) have demonstrated strong capabilities in SMILES-based molecule generation, and reinforcement learning (RL) is often employed to fine-tune these models to optimize desired molecular properties \cite{mokaya2023testing,korshunova2022generative}.
% To further discover molecules with desired biological or drug-likeness properties, researchers have increasingly turned to reinforcement learning (RL) to fine-tune pre-trained models \cite{mokaya2023testing}.
