\subsection{Individual Metrics vs Collective Metrics}
An individual's performance in a game is accounted by the in-game performance metrics of the individual player such as \emph{gold earned, champion experience, kills}, etc. Correlation analysis and variance inflation factor tests revealed multi-collinearity between in-game metrics which were thus removed from the feature set. Additionally, the graph metrics i.e. \emph{player in-degree} and \emph{player out-degree} which signify the overall receipt and provision of all assists for a single individual player is also accounted towards individual metrics. Thus, the individual metrics include -- \emph{vision score per minute, gold earned per minute, champion experience per minute, player in-degree} and \emph{player out-degree}. The collective metrics are composed of average values of in-game performance metrics of the players in a team along with the graph metrics that signify the overall flow of assistance within the team. Thus, the collective metrics consist of \emph{average team gold earned per minute, average team vision score per minute, average team champion experience per minute, team in-degree centrality, team out-degree centrality} and \emph{effective graph resistance}.

\subsection{Exploratory Factor Analysis} 
Prior work by Mora-Cantallops and Sicilia have confirmed that gold earned is one of the most important factors determining the game outcome~\cite{mora2019team}. Gold is earned by all players at a steady rate throughout the game in addition to champions and minion kills, tower takedowns and other objectives. However, only using gold earned as a feature doesn't identify the nature of intelligent behaviors exhibited by individuals and teams. Therefore, exploratory factor analysis is performed to uncover latent factor variables that explain the nature of intelligent interactions between individuals and teams. The optimal number of explanatory factors for individuals and teams are identified via Scree analysis. Factor loading values of the latent factors infer the nature of interactions in both individuals and teams i.e. \emph{acquiring} vs \emph{sharing} factor and \emph{cooperative} vs \emph{non-cooperative} factor.

\subsection{Machine Learning Algorithms} 
\textbf{Classification:} The winning outcome of a game in most cases is determined either by the exceptional performances of an individual player or by the collective efforts of a team. The explanatory factors that suggest the nature of intelligent behaviours in individuals and teams are then input as features to predict the winning outcome of the game using different classification algorithms i.e., binary logistic regression, decision tree classifier, random forest classifier, and XGBoost classifier. Classification results are evaluated in terms of accuracy, f1-score and area under the curve metrics. To accept or reject \textbf{H1}, the evaluation scores by the collective performance factors are compared against individual performance factors.

\noindent \\ \textbf{Clustering:} Further analysis involves profiling individual players and teams based on the explanatory factors into three distinct clusters for each. K-means clustering algorithm is used to categorize individual players and teams into their respective clusters. Based on the average values of the performance factors within each cluster, we manually add labels to these clusters. The frequency of wins (or winning rate) within such distinct clusters of players and teams are then identified. Cross tabulation analysis enables how often a team cluster with a majority of particular type of players win the game. Comparison of the winning rates of such player and teams clusters via cross-tabulation analysis provides further rigour to accept or reject the hypothesis \textbf{H1}.

\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
Three main results are illustrated in this paper -- 
\subsection{Individual Acquisition but Collective Cooperation} \label{sec:result1}
Exploratory factor analysis on the individual and team collective metrics reveal latent factors which relate to \emph{acquiring} vs \emph{sharing} of resources in individuals and \emph{cooperative} vs \emph{non-cooperative} behaviors in teams. The optimal number of factors for both individual and collective team performance metrics were identified to be $N=2$ via Scree analysis. Based on the factor loading values of the latent factors, such factors could be interpreted as either \emph{acquiring} or \emph{sharing} of resources in individuals, and as either \emph{cooperative} or \emph{non-cooperative} factors in teams. Table~\ref{tab:factor_loadings} outlines the factor loading values of underlying components for both individual and teams. Upon training the match dataset represented by such latent factors at both the individual and team-level to predict the winning outcome using different classification algorithms, the feature importance values for such latent factors present an interesting dynamic between individual and collective behaviour i.e., individuals attempt to win games via acquisition of resources for a player whereas teams attempt to win games via cooperative, altruistic behaviors. Figure~\ref{fig:relative_importance_scores} presents the feature importance values of the latent factors at the individual and collective team-level across different sets of classification algorithms.

