\subsection{Highly-skilled players exhibit higher levels of collective intelligence} \label{sec:result3}
To test for \textbf{H2}, we investigated the association of graph-based collective intelligence metrics with the average rank of a team. An in-game matchmaking algorithm ensures that players having similar skill-levels are often matched together as teammates or opponents. Players who play the game competitively are rewarded with \textit{League Points} which establishes a hierarchy of players based on a two-tiered ranking system of \textit{tiers} and \textit{divisions} (see \textit{Appendix}). Players who excel in the game are signified by a higher positioning in the ranking ladder. We find that highly-skilled players (and thus teams) as signified by the higher rankings within the server, exhibit higher levels of collective intelligence compared to less-skilled teams i.e. teams in ranking tiers such as \emph{Diamond} and above demonstrate higher levels of cooperation and flow of resources within their team. Therefore, higher ranking teams have lower values for effective graph resistance as well as lower in-degree and out-degree centrality compared to lower ranking teams, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:ranking_violin_plots}. This difference in the levels these graph-based collective intelligence metrics across different rankings is found to be statistically significant by Kruskal-Wallis test (i.e. \emph{p-val} = $1.08 \times 10\textsuperscript{-33}, 6.02 \times 10\textsuperscript{-157}$ and  $1.63 \times 10\textsuperscript{-34}$ for in-degree centrality, out-degree centrality and effective graph resistance respectively). 

