\section{Discussion}\label{discussion}
In this paper, we argue and present evidence for the statement -- \emph{collective intelligence outperforms individual talent} via a case study in \emph{League of Legends}. By analyzing the frequency of wins, the results show that acting on the collective interest of a group leads to a higher likelihood of winning games rather than relying on individual skills and abilities. Moreover, we demonstrate that cooperative teams with low performing individuals outperform non-cooperative teams with high performing individuals. 

Previous work by Mora-Cantallops and Sicilia report that centralization of resources is detrimental to team efficiency and performance~\cite{mora2019team}, and our findings align with it. However, unlike this previous work in which the graph metrics -- \emph{team in-degree centrality} and \emph{team out-degree centrality} were computed based on \emph{kills} assistance, our work accounts for all forms of assists (in securing game objectives) including a tailored measure of passive assistance in \emph{positional map-pressure}. Likewise, this work is the first study in which effective graph resistance is modeled to study team networks in \emph{League of Legends}. Another work by Kim et. al~\cite{kim2015work} analyze group collective intelligence in controlled participants, however this work incorporates a large-scale participation of anonymous, more representative set of players, thereby enhancing the validity and general applicability of the findings. In their work, they show that higher \emph{tier}ed teams exhibit higher levels of collective intelligence which is congruent with our findings where we present statistically significant differences in the levels of collective intelligence across teams of different \emph{tier} rankings. Additional concerns regarding the validity of our work is clarified in \emph{Appendix}.

In our results, we find that non-cooperative teams having a majority of sharing (or low-performing) individuals win 50\% of the games (see Table~\ref{tab:cross_analysis_winning_rate}). However, the observed number games for such non-cooperative teams is significantly low ($N=2$). Thus, the winning rate statistic for this particular case can be misleading. To remedy such issues, a larger dataset could be collected and analyzed. As we demonstrate a dominating winning ratio of cooperative teams with low-performing individuals over non-cooperative teams with high-performing individuals in head-to-head matches, it would be interesting to identify the distribution of such winning ratio across different \emph{tier} rankings. Additionally, a compelling study could be carried out whereby random, specific individuals are profiled for matches where they display exceptional performances in non-cooperative teams as well as mediocre performances in cooperative teams, and how often such displays can be attributed to wins, and their significance in climbing up the ranking ladder. As Krause et al.~\cite{krause2011swarm} and Santos et al.~\cite{santos2012role} advocate diversity for increased collective performance, the diversity in the types of \emph{champions} picked in game, can be studied to extend this work.

The phenomenon of collective intelligence in multiplayer online gaming environments merits investigation into diverse gaming genres, for instance in First-Person Shooter (FPS) games, Massive Multi-player Online Role Playing Games (MMORPGs). Unlike FPS games (which mostly focus on individual reflex and mechanics) and MMORPGs (in which games can span over a longer time period), the authors chose to study collective intelligence in MOBA games due to their well-defined team structures, player roles, resource and objective trade-offs that bear similarities to collective actions in real-world problems. Specifically, the authors chose to conduct this study in \emph{League of Legends} because -- (a) it is the most popular MOBA game played worldwide, (b) features API endpoints that allow access to large-scale structured data, and (c) personally due to the authors' familiarity and inclination towards the game. The framework that is presented in this work can also be used in other MOBA games like DOTA 2 to quantify the levels of outperformance. However, implementation will require tweaking the source code (data structures and API calls).

On a broader perspective, our results present the contradictory dynamics observed in the behavior of an individual when optimizing for self vs when optimizing for the collective. Such dynamic behaviors can also be observed in the design of sustainable infrastructure products. For instance, individuals can be tempted to use non-sustainable products which are inexpensive (immediate lower negative payoff) compared to eco-friendly products which can be costly (higher negative payoff) to implement but over a long run offers a greater benefit to the entire population~\cite{asikis2021value}. Insights presented in this study can thus stipulate independent individuals to prioritize collective altruistic behaviors over individual predisposition in complex social systems. Furthermore, collective efforts can be crucial to attain the desired goals not just within online gaming ecosystems, but also in other more fundamentally critical aspects of our online (as well as offline) existence. For instance, collective efforts can help restrict the misinformation that is rampant in online social networks. Policies and regulations set up by the decentralized and collective efforts of the population in socio-economic systems such as consumer markets, governments, etc. prevent monopoly and abuse of authority resulting in products and systems that are favorable for the whole collective.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
In this paper, we explored the presence of collective intelligence among players in the MOBA game, \emph{League of Legends}. Our findings conclude that collective intelligence characterized by sharing of resources, flow of information and cooperative behavior outperforms individual intelligence. Our results reiterate the paradoxical imperative for collaboration and cooperation in highly competitive environments for achieving optimal outcomes. Further work complementing this research can be done by testing for the performance of collective intelligence and individual intelligence in real-world applications beyond gaming environments.


