\subsection{Resources: Gold, Experience and Vision}
Experience is a metric that enables players to increase their Champion’s level, in turn, increasing the Champion’s strength. Experience is primarily gained by farming minions or jungle camps. However, it is not imperative that a player kills the minion to be awarded experience. Gold is used by players to purchase in-game items, making their champion stronger. Gold is one of the most important factors which contributes towards winning the game. Gaining gold and buying items enables a champion to carry their lead forward by helping their team achieve additional objectives. Gold is earned in various ways -- passively at a steady rate of time, scoring kills on enemy champions, last-hit on minions and elite-monster kills, destroying enemy tower structures, etc. Vision is a valuable resource measured by ‘vision score’. However, unlike gold or experience, no advantage is given to the player in terms of strength or currency. Players spend their gold to purchase \emph{wards} – these items are placed strategically on the map to reveal dark areas that cannot be seen otherwise. These dark areas may reveal the position of an enemy player, thus, yielding a strategic advantage to the other team. Moreover, players can destroy wards placed by the opposing team – denying them such information. Doing these actions (purchasing, placing and destroying wards) increases their vision score.

\subsection{Turrets and Elite-Monsters}
Turrets (also known as towers), are positioned in every lane and surround each teams’ base. Turrets will attack all enemy characters within their range, one at a time. Players gain gold by attacking and destroying the opposing teams’ turrets. Destruction of an enemy turret is an example of achieving an objective. Turrets can also be destroyed by minions, in which case players receive no monetary benefit. Elite monsters are considered common objectives as they do not belong to a team, spawning mid-way at the rift. All players in the game look to kill them and reap their benefits. A small amount of gold is rewarded for securing elite monsters objectives. However, their main benefit is improving the base statistics of players (of the team that secure the objective) such as movement speed, healing rate, abilities cool-down, etc, which may be temporary or over the duration of the entire game.

\subsection{Kills and Assists}
A player is awarded with a kill score if the player uses their abilities or spells such that an enemy's health points is reduced to zero. A player scoring a kill on enemy champion is rewarded with a significant amount of gold. A player scores an assist when the player helps a team-mate score a kill on an enemy champion. Assists are also awarded for tower destruction and elite-monster kills. Assistance can be in the form of dealing damage to enemy champions or providing utility effects such as healing, shielding, etc. to ally champions that results in the kill. Players receive a small amount of gold when providing assists.

\subsection{Positional map-pressure assist} \label{app:mappressure}
A new tailored metric called the ``positional map-pressure" assist, which reflects how a player within a team exhibits intelligent map positioning thereby resulting in an opponent kill, is computed for each match. Figure~\ref{fig:map_pressure} depicts how positional map-pressure can occur in-game. Opponent kill assists, tower destruction assists or elite monsters kill assists by a given player can be directly retrieved by processing the \emph{match timeline} data. However, positional \emph{map-pressure} assist is not directly accounted within the game. As such, by using the match timeline data, positioning of all team players an opponent kill event were identified, and a player was awarded a positional map-pressure assist if the player's position was within a specified radius during the kill event.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.3]{positional_map_pressure.png}
    \caption{An instance of gameplay on the Summoner's Rift map in which an opponent kill is scored due to positional map-pressure. Blue team player A is attempting to score an opponent kill on red team player Y, while Y attempts to flee. Another blue team player B obstructs Y from escaping, thus inciting Y to engage in a fight resulting in a kill score for blue. While B does not actively assist A in the kill process, B's positioning induces map pressure causing Y's death. B is awarded map-pressure assist if B is positioned within a specified radius during the kill event.}
    \label{fig:map_pressure}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Ranking System and League Points} \label{app:ranking}
The ranking system used in League of Legends employs a two-tiered structured ladder designed to measure and reward player skill through competitive gameplay in ranked matches. The ranking system consists of ten \emph{tiers}: \emph{Iron, Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum, Emerald, Diamond, Master, Grandmaster,} and \emph{Challenger}. Each tier below \emph{Master} is subdivided into four \emph{divisions} (for example, \emph{Gold IV} to \emph{Gold I}), with IV being the lowest and I the highest within a tier.

\emph{League Points} (LP) are the core mechanism that enable progression in the ranking system. Players earn LP by winning matches and lose LP upon defeat. In each sub-division of tiers below \emph{Master}, LP are ranged between 0 and 100, such that upon exceeding 100 LP for a given sub-division within a tier, the player is promoted to the next immediate division (or the next tier if current division is I) and LP is reset to 0. For promotion in tiers above \emph{Master}, there is no fixed rule, but rather depends on pool size of players in the upper tiers as well as higher amount of LP is required. The amount of LP gained or lost per match is influenced by several factors, including a hidden matchmaking Rating (MMR), which reflects a player's skill level relative to their rank. Higher MMR results in larger LP gains for victories and smaller losses for defeats

\subsection{Data Retrieval Process} \label{app:dataretrieval}
Data on \emph{League of Legends} ranked matches played on any of the official server maintained by Riot Games is freely accessible via a set of RESTful API endpoints. Figure~\ref{fig:api_calls_process} shows the overall data-retrieval process used in the study. An initial set of 10 (one for each ranking tier) random, hand-picked summoner names are stored in a text file as seed data. A python script reads this seed configuration file and makes the following API calls --
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{summonerName} endpoint: This API endpoint fetches the corresponding unique identifier, called the puuid, for a given summoner name.
    \item \textbf{puuid}/ids endpoint: This API endpoint returns a list of match identifiers for the most recent N-games (configurable) played by a player with a given puuid.
    \item \textbf{matchid} endpoint: This API endpoint fetches the details of a given match id for each player in both teams such as - gold earned, items crafted, experience gained, kills scored, champion roles and levels, etc. Details of each match is saved as a JSON flatfile.
    \item \textbf{matchid}/timeline endpoint: This API endpoint fetches the details of various game event (such as kills, level-ups, items purchase, etc.) as it occurs chronologically over the duration of the game with a given match id. Timeline details of each match id is saved as a JSON flatfile.
    \item entries/by-summoner/\textbf{summonerId} endpoint: This API endpoint retrieves the rank information data for a given summoner identified by their unique puuid.
\end{itemize}

The client script thus reads the initial seed summoner names and writes match data and match timeline files in the local directory. Such files are merged and data filters, concerning game-type, duration, disconnection, etc. are applied to remove inconsistent rows.

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.4]{lol_api_calls.png}
    \caption{Data retrieval process via RESTful APIs provided by Riot Games}
    \label{fig:api_calls_process}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Threats to Validity} \label{app:threatsvalidity}
There may be concerns of initial selection bias when handpicking the seed \emph{summonerNames} used in the data collection process. However, we ensure that the additional fetch retrieves data for players that are not in the initial list. In 50\% of the dataset, it is ensured that a single unique player has played no more than 2 games, resulting in a total of 164K unique players. Thus, any selection bias over the initially selected 10 seed summoners is unlikely to impact the results. Additionally, the collected dataset features games played exclusively within the European servers. \emph{League of Legends} is played on different servers across different geographical locations i.e., America, Europe, Asia and Oceania, with notable differences in players' pool size and skills. However, we expect data from different servers to exhibit similar pattern of results. In-game metrics such as \emph{towers destroyed, elite monster kills, enemy champion kills} and \emph{minion kills} are highly correlated with \emph{gold earned}. Thus, these features are removed. Variance inflation test yields values of $VIF > 5$ for the feature \emph{gold earned}, which signifies high correlation with other features. However, it was not removed from the feature set as it is the most contributing feature for the acquiring factor. The suitability of factor analysis at the individual and collective levels yield KMO values of $0.57$ and $0.61$ respectively, which is poor but acceptable. Our results demonstrate that the winning rate for a cooperative team with a majority of resource acquiring players is as high as 94\%. However this may be misinterpreted. It does not mean that the same cooperative team with the same set of high-performing individuals will win 94\% of their games. This is because the Riot Games' matchmaking algorithm ensures that as players win games, they are positioned higher in the ranking ladder, as a result they are matched with relatively more-skilled, difficult opponents when winning, and ensuring that most players roughly win 50\% of their games. Finally, since the outcome of a match is a zero-sum game (no draws are applicable for a match), a team can lose not because it is bad, but the opponent has a small advantage over the team. On the other hand, a non-cooperative team can also win exploiting a blunder from the opponents. Such extreme cases have not been handled in the dataset.

