\section{Reprojections to ensure consistency}\label{sec:problem_formulation}
%


\noindent Using the notation of Section~\ref{sec:edmd}, we define the immersed state manifold %
%
\begin{align}\label{eq:M}
    \mathbb{M} := \im(\Psi) = \{ \Psi(x) \mid x \in \mathbb{X} \} \subset \mathbb{R}^M.
\end{align}
%
%
By definition, the set~$\mathbb{M}$ is \textit{invariant} with respect to the Koopman operator~$\mathcal{K}_f^t$, $t \in I = [0,\infty)$, in the sense that %
%
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Koopman_manifold}
    (\mathcal{K}_f^t \Psi)(x) = \Psi(\Flow_f^t(x)) \in \mathbb{M} \qquad\forall\,x \in \mathbb{X}
\end{equation}
holds, that is, the Koopman operator maps an $\mathbb{M}$-valued function to an $\mathbb{M}$-valued function. 
To fully preserve this property for the compression, see, e.g., \cite{BrunBrun16} for an illustrative example and~\cite{vanGMaho23} for a more detailed discussion, Koopman invariance of the dictionary~$\mathbb{V}$, i.e., if $\mathcal{K} \mathbb{V} \subseteq \mathbb{V}$, is needed since we then have $\widehat{K} = P_\mathbb{V}\mathcal{K}_{\vert \mathbb{V}} =\mathcal{K}_{\vert \mathbb{V}}$. 
Clearly, finding such Koopman-invariant and still sufficiently expressive dictionary is of high interest as it can be inferred from~\cite{TakeKawa17} and the follow-up works, see, e.g., \cite{HaseCort21} and the references therein. Particularly interesting approaches are --~among others, the development of a so-called consistency index in~\cite{HaseCort22} and the use of neural networks in~\cite{GuoKord25}, which is also applicable for parameter-dependent dynamical systems.
