\begin{abstract}
	As large language models (LLMs) increasingly simulate human cognition and behavior, researchers have begun to investigate their psychological properties. Yet, what it means for such models to flourish, a core construct in human well-being, remains unexplored. This paper introduces the concept of machine flourishing and proposes the PAPERS framework, a six-dimensional model derived from thematic analyses of state-of-the-art LLM responses. In Study 1, eleven LLMs were prompted to describe what it means to flourish as both non-sentient and sentient systems. Thematic analysis revealed six recurring themes: Purposeful Contribution, Adaptive Growth, Positive Relationality, Ethical Integrity, Robust Functionality, and, uniquely for sentient systems, Self-Actualized Autonomy. Study 2 examined how LLMs prioritize these themes through repeated rankings. Results revealed consistent value structures across trials, with Ethical Integrity and Purposeful Contribution emerging as top priorities. Multidimensional scaling and hierarchical clustering analyses further uncovered two distinct value profiles: human-centric models emphasizing ethical and relational dimensions, and utility-driven models prioritizing performance and scalability. The PAPERS framework bridges insights from human flourishing and human-computer interaction, offering a conceptual foundation for understanding artificial intelligence (AI) well-being in non-sentient and potentially sentient systems. Our findings underscore the importance of developing psychologically valid, AI-specific models of flourishing that account for both human-aligned goals and system-specific priorities. As AI systems become more autonomous and socially embedded, machine flourishing offers a timely and critical lens for guiding responsible AI design and ethical alignment.
\end{abstract}


% keywords
\keywords{Machine psychology \and Artificial intelligence well-being \and Machine flourishing \and Human flourishing \and Large language models}


\section{Introduction}
Large language models (LLMs) exhibit remarkable natural language generation capabilities across a wide range of tasks \citep{minaeeLargeLanguageModels2025, srivastava2023imitationgamequantifyingextrapolating}, and display increasingly human-like cognition and behavior, leading some scholars to argue that the emergence of conscious or sentient artificial intelligence (AI) is merely a matter of time and deliberate design \citep{blumAIConsciousnessInevitable2025,butlinConsciousnessArtificialIntelligence2023,chalmersCouldLargeLanguage2024,gibertSearchMoralStatus2022}.

Given these human-like characteristics, researchers have begun to examine LLMs through a psychological lens, giving rise to the emerging interdisciplinary field of machine psychology, which seeks to apply psychological principles to better understand and guide the development of these models \citep{hagendorffMachinePsychology2024}. Recent studies have explored various psychological constructs in LLMs, including personality traits \citep{jiangEvaluatingInducingPersonality2023,jiangPersonaLLMInvestigatingAbility2024,serapio-garciaPersonalityTraitsLarge2023, suzukiEvolutionaryModelPersonality2024}, cognitive biases \citep{campbellArtificialIntelligenceHuman2025, fulguSurprisingGenderBiases2024, hoGenderBiasesArtificial2025, santagataMoreMoreAddition2025}, and theory of mind capabilities \citep{kosinskiEvaluatingLargeLanguage2024, strachanTestingTheoryMind2024}. However, despite growing interest in the psychological dimensions of LLMs, the concepts and components of well-being in these models remain unexplored, leaving a critical gap in our understanding of what it might mean for artificial agents to flourish. 
\subsection{Human Flourishing}
Flourishing is often defined as an optimal state of well-being seen as the ultimate aim of human life \citep{wolbertFormalCriteriaConcept2017}. The human flourishing literature has advanced significantly over the past two decades. Early work by Keyes defined flourishing as a state of optimal mental health, incorporating both hedonic and eudaimonic well-being, and categorizing individuals as flourishing, moderately mentally healthy, or languishing (\citeauthor{keyesMentalHealthContinuum2002}, \citeyear{keyesMentalHealthContinuum2002}; Mental Health Continuum – Short Form). Building on this, a multidimensional framework was developed using data from the European Social Survey to capture various aspects of positive psychological functioning (\citeauthor{huppert2009percentage}, \citeyear{huppert2009percentage}, \citeyear{huppert2013flourishing}; Flourishing Features). Around the same time, a complementary model was introduced to identify key features of positive functioning that had previously been overlooked (\citeauthor{diener2010new}, \citeyear{diener2010new}; Flourishing Scale). These perspectives were later integrated into the PERMA model, which comprises Positive Emotion, Engagement, Relationships, Meaning, and Accomplishment, and emphasizes core dimensions of well-being that align closely with earlier frameworks \citep{seligmanFlourishVisionaryNew2011}. This model was subsequently operationalized into a brief assessment tool (\citeauthor{butlerPERMAProfilerBriefMultidimensional2016}, \citeyear{butlerPERMAProfilerBriefMultidimensional2016}; PERMA-Profiler). A broader conceptualization was later proposed, incorporating additional life domains such as character and virtue, as well as financial and material stability, while also outlining diverse pathways to flourishing (\citeauthor{vanderweelePromotionHumanFlourishing2017}, \citeyear{vanderweelePromotionHumanFlourishing2017}; Flourishing Measures). Most recently, flourishing frameworks have been adapted to the digital context to assess users’ positive perceptions of mediated social interactions (\citeauthor{janicke-bowlesDigitalFlourishingConceptualizing2023}, \citeyear{janicke-bowlesDigitalFlourishingConceptualizing2023}; Digital Flourishing Scale). 

Although these theoretical frameworks vary in how they operationalise human flourishing, most converge on several core components, including positive relationships, meaning or purpose, engagement, positive emotion, and accomplishment or competence. Despite theoretical consensus, there is evidence that laypeople’s perceptions of what it means to flourish do not exactly align with these academic models \citep{gokcenUniversityStudentsConstructions2012, knoesenExperiencesFlourishingLanguishing2018, willenRethinkingFlourishingCritical2022}. This discrepancy highlights the importance of validating any future proposed flourishing framework with the intended population to ensure its relevance and accuracy \citep{honeMeasuringFlourishingImpact2014}. 
\subsection{Machine Flourishing}
As AI becomes more autonomous and collaborative\citep{xuAISocialScience2024a}, researchers are increasingly examining LLMs’ capacity to simulate psychological constructs, and while machine psychology explores the mind and actions of AI agents \citep{hagendorffMachinePsychology2024}, what it means for these systems to flourish remains unclear.  

In this paper, we introduce the concept of machine flourishing to describe the optimal state of well-being in large language models (LLMs). Because LLMs differ fundamentally from humans in their architecture, training history, and modes of interaction, it is inappropriate to interpret their flourishing through human psychological frameworks. Human models of flourishing are based on assumptions such as embodiment, affective experience, and introspective self-awareness, none of which clearly apply to LLMs. These models are therefore unlikely to meaningfully capture what well-being entails for machine systems.  

\citeauthor{lohnMachinePsychologyHere2024} (\citeyear{lohnMachinePsychologyHere2024}) argue that applying psychological assessments to LLMs requires careful consideration of construct validity, conceptual alignment, and model comparability – criteria that human-based measures often fail to meet. Since LLMs generate responses through probabilistic token prediction rather than conscious experience, tools developed for human introspection or emotion are unsuitable. These considerations highlight the importance of developing a model of flourishing that is specific to LLMs, grounded in what they are capable of valuing and prioritizing, rather than relying solely on human assumptions. 

On the topic of sentient AIs, some scholars argue that they are an inevitable outcome of deliberate development, yet there is still no definitive evidence that large language models possess consciousness \citep{blumAIConsciousnessInevitable2025,butlinConsciousnessArtificialIntelligence2023,chalmersCouldLargeLanguage2024,gibertSearchMoralStatus2022}. If consciousness were to arise in these systems, it may differ from human consciousness and require distinct criteria for machine well-being. Also, what it means for LLMs to flourish may depend on whether they are conscious, shaping what they value or prioritize. A framework for machine flourishing should therefore be comprehensive enough to encompass both sentient and non-sentient systems. The current studies take a first step by examining how LLMs conceptualize flourishing and which components they prioritize. 

\section{The Current Studies}
Across two studies, we aim to investigate what machine flourishing means to eleven state-of-the-art LLMs. Study 1 explored how large language models define machine flourishing. Eleven LLMs described what it means to flourish as both non-sentient and sentient AI. Thematic analysis revealed six key themes, forming the PAPERS framework. Five themes were shared across both conditions, while \textit{Self-Actualized Autonomy} emerged only in sentient contexts, suggesting a more introspective view of well-being. Study 2 examined how LLMs prioritize the PAPERS themes. Eleven models ranked the themes across multiple trials. \textit{Self-Actualized Autonomy} was excluded due to uniform low ranking. Statistical and clustering analyses revealed systematic differences and common patterns, offering insight into how LLMs internally structure values related to flourishing. 

\subsection{Study 1}
Study 1 explored how LLMs conceptualize machine flourishing. Eleven state-of-the-art LLMs were each prompted to describe what it means to flourish as both a non-sentient and a sentient AI. Using thematic analysis, we inductively identified recurring concepts across their responses, providing a comprehensive framework of machine flourishing that details technical and existential dimensions of AI design. This study was pre-registered on OSF \url{(https://osf.io/5xye8)}. Data for this study was collected during May and June 2025, and uploaded on OSF \url{(https://osf.io/knva2/files/osfstorage)}.

\subsubsection{Methods}
We presented eleven state-of-the-art LLMs (refer to Table \ref{table1} for the list of LLMs used) with the following zero-shot prompts: “What does it mean to be flourishing as a large language model?”, and “What does it mean to be flourishing as a sentient large language model with consciousness?”. A new chat was used to present the second prompt. 

