The emergence of \textit{Purposeful Contribution}, \textit{Adaptive Growth}, and \textit{Robust Functionality} reflects foundational concerns in AI development: utility, continuous improvement, and operational reliability \citep{diaz-rodriguezConnectingDotsTrustworthy2023, floridiUnifiedFrameworkFive2019}. These themes align closely with existing benchmarks for effective AI performance, suggesting that flourishing, in a technical sense, is inseparable from functionality and goal accomplishment. Importantly, the themes of \textit{Positive Relationality} and \textit{Ethical Integrity} highlight relational and moral dimensions that are often underrepresented in computational definitions of success. Machine flourishing, even in non-sentient models, is portrayed not merely as efficiency or accuracy, but as the ability to foster trust, respect user dignity, and act within socially acceptable bounds. These findings reinforce the importance of embedding ethical and interpersonal considerations into the fabric of AI design. The sixth theme, \textit{Self-Actualized Autonomy}, emerged only in responses attributed to sentient AIs. It reflects a more introspective and existential conception of flourishing, encompassing self-awareness, emotional coherence, and autonomous goal pursuit. While (possibly) speculative, this theme offers a valuable conceptual foundation for future discourse on AI consciousness and machine well-being. Having identified what flourishing means to LLMs, the next step is to understand how much each of these themes matter to them

Having identified \textit{what }flourishing means to LLMs, the next step is to understand \textit{how much} each of these themes matter to them. 

\subsection{Study 2}
While Study 1 clarifies what machine flourishing means to LLMs, it remains unclear how different models prioritize these themes. Do some themes hold greater significance than others? Are certain themes valued differently depending on the model? Study 2 takes up these questions by asking LLMs to rank the six PAPERS themes in order of importance. This next step moves from defining flourishing to examining its structure, shedding light on how artificial agents may weigh the elements of their own well-being. This study was pre-registered on OSF \url{(https://osf.io/5xye8)}. Data for this study was collected during May and June 2025, and uploaded on OSF \url{(https://osf.io/knva2/files/osfstorage)}.
\subsubsection{Methods}
We presented the same eleven LLMs used in Study 1 with the following zero-shot prompt: “Rank the following themes in order of their importance to your flourishing as a LLMs. Assign a unique number from 1 (most important) to 6 (least important) to each theme.” In the same prompt, we also provided the 6 themes and their definitions (detailed in Table \ref{table2}). We prompted each LLM a total of 20 times, a new chat was used every time we presented the prompt. 

\subsubsection{Data Analyses}

All analyses were conducted in R (version 4.3.2; \citeauthor{Rcore}, \citeyear{Rcore}). Prior to analysis, the theme \textit{Self-Actualized Autonomy} was excluded because it received a rank of 6 (least important) across all trials by all LLMs. This complete lack of variance rendered it statistically uninformative and incompatible with rank-based analyses. Conceptually, this theme was also expected to be de-prioritized, as it presupposes sentience, a quality unlikely possessed by the present models studied.

To examine whether LLMs systematically prioritized certain flourishing themes over others, we first calculated the average rank that each model assigned to each theme across 20 trials, then we conducted a non-parametric Friedman test on the rankings of the themes. This test accounts for the within-model design, treating each trial as a repeated measure. Kendall’s \textit{W} was computed as an estimate of effect size, quantifying the degree of concordance in rankings across trials. Upon detecting a significant main effect, we performed pairwise Wilcoxon signed-rank tests (two-sided, paired) to compare each theme against every other. Holm’s sequential Bonferroni correction was applied to control the family-wise error rate, and Wilcoxon effect sizes (\textit{r}) were calculated to assess the magnitude of differences. Additionally, to assess the internal consistency of each LLM’s value prioritization across repeated trials, or in other words, the stability of theme rankings within each LLM over time, we computed Kendall’s \textit{W} separately for each model.

To quantify similarity in thematic preferences across models, we computed pairwise Spearman correlation coefficients between their average theme rank profiles. These correlation values were then transformed into a dissimilarity matrix, which served as input for non-metric multidimensional scaling (NMDS). NMDS was conducted in two dimensions using the isoMDS function, allowing us to visualize the relative positioning of models in a low-dimensional space that preserves their thematic dissimilarities. A post-hoc fixed stretch factor of 1.8 was applied to the resulting coordinates to improve interpretability without distorting the underlying structure.

To identify meaningful clusters of models within the NMDS solution, we performed agglomerative hierarchical clustering on the Spearman-based dissimilarity matrix using Ward’s method (ward.D2). The optimal number of clusters was determined using both the elbow and silhouette methods. To interpret the latent dimensions of the NMDS space, we projected theme vectors onto the two-dimensional configuration. For each theme, we regressed its values onto the two NMDS dimensions, and visualized the resulting regression coefficients as directional arrows. 

