\section{Simulations}\label{chap:simulation}
This section presents simulations to validate the theoretical results and highlight the efficacy of the proposed algorithms. We illustrate the threshold-based behavior of the optimal policy and demonstrate the superior performance of our proposed algorithm compared to heuristic algorithms under perfect channel information. Next, we extend the analysis to unknown channel models, where structural updates achieve faster convergence and higher rewards during training compared to standard methods. The results collectively confirm the monotonicity and superadditivity properties derived earlier while highlighting the efficacy of the proposed algorithms.

\subsection{Simulation Setup}\label{chap:sim setup}
In this section, we examine the following systems~\cite{ren2018attack}:
\begin{align*}
A_1 &= \begin{bmatrix}1.2&0.2\\0.3&1\end{bmatrix}, \quad A_2 = \begin{bmatrix}1.2&0.15\\0&1.1\end{bmatrix},\\
Q_1 &= \begin{bmatrix}2&0\\0&1\end{bmatrix}, \quad Q_2 = \begin{bmatrix}1&0.5\\0.5&0.5\end{bmatrix},
\end{align*}
and $C_1=[1,0]$, $C_1=[1,0.2]$, $R_1=1$ and  $R_2=3$.

Unless otherwise specified, we limit the range of $\tau^{(i)}$ to a finite set $\{0,\dots,20\}$ and assume normalized transmission power with $\mathcal{P}=\{0,1\}$ and ${\rm SINR}_k^{(i)}={H_k^{(i)}}/{(5G_k^{(i)}p_k^{(i)}+\sigma_i^2)}$. Additionally, we assume that the channels $H_k^{(i)}$, and $G_k^{(i)}$ have two possible states: $s_1 = 0.02$ and $s_2 = 0.09$, with transition probabilities $t^s_{s_1,s_1}=t^s_{s_2,s_2}=0.8$ and $t^s_{s_1,s_2}=t^s_{s_2,s_1}=0.2$. For the modulation scheme, we employ a QAM scheme (Example~\ref{exam:qam}) with a parameter value of $b=0.5$. The noise level is represented by $\sigma_i=0.2$. For the energy harvester, we consider $b_{\max} = 3$ and $\mathcal{E}=\{0,1,2\}$, where the transition probability is provided below~\cite{leong2018transmission}:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{bmatrix}
t^e_{0,0} & t^e_{0,1}& t^e_{0,2}\\
t^e_{1,0} & t^e_{1,1}& t^e_{1,2}\\
t^e_{2,0} & t^e_{2,1}& t^e_{2,2}
\end{bmatrix}=
\begin{bmatrix}
0.2 &0.3 &0.5\\
0.3 &0.4 &0.3\\
0.1 &0.2 &0.7
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation*}
