\subsection{Results for Unknown Channel Model}
1) {\it\bf Structural Update}: To better visualize the learning process of the $Q$-factor, we herein restrict our focus to the case where the communication channel is fixed and $\tau^{(i)}\in\{0,\dots,5\}$. Fig.~\ref{fig:Q} illustrates the evolution of the $Q$-factor under both standard~\eqref{eq:q learning} and structural~\eqref{eq:primal}-\eqref{eq:dual} updates. Both algorithms exhibit convergence, with the structural version demonstrating faster convergence. Furthermore, Fig.~\ref{fig:Q} reveals that the standard updates do not consistently satisfy the properties of monotonicity (Lemma~\ref{lemma:monototic q}) and superadditivity (Lemma~\ref{lemma:super}), whereas the structural updates largely adhere to these properties, which accounts for its faster convergence.

2) {\it\bf Average Rewards}: Fig.~\ref{fig:reward} shows the average reward for the original setup described in Section~\ref{chap:sim setup}. While the final average rewards of both algorithms converge to the same value, the structural updates yield higher rewards during the learning process compared with the standard one.


\section{Conclusion}\label{chap:conclusion}
In this paper, we analyzed jamming attacks against remote state estimation. The focus was on developing algorithms to determine optimal jamming policies in the context of the ``harvest and jam'' scenario. Two cases were studied based on the attacker's knowledge of the system: (i) perfect channel knowledge and (ii) unknown channel model. We demonstrated that the proposed algorithms for both cases converge to the optimal policy asymptotically. Furthermore, the optimal policy was shown to exhibit certain structural properties that can be leveraged to expedite both algorithms. Simulation results validated the optimality of the obtained policies. Additionally, the accelerated version of the algorithms exhibited faster convergence and yielded higher average rewards during the learning process compared to the standard algorithm. Future work could extend this analysis by exploring a game-theoretic framework where sensors are aware of the attacker.


