\section{Background and Motivation}\label{sec:background}
\subsection{Statevector-based Simulation}
Statevector-based simulation keeps track of all \emph{amplitudes} in the statevector. An amplitude is a complex number, and an $n$-qubit statevector is a length-$2^n$ complex vector with norm 1. %Statevectors represent \emph{pure} states. This paper focuses exclusively on pure states, as the extension to mixed states is straightforward by expressing mixed states as linear combinations of pure states. We use the little-endian notation, where less significant bits are positioned on the right.
%We adopt the little-Indian notation, wherein less significant bits are written to the right.
Let $\psi$ be the length-$2^n$ complex vector that represents an $n$-qubit statevector. Applying a single-qubit gate \gU{} on qubit $k$ is equivalently performing the matrix-vector multiplication specified by 
\begin{equation}
    \big(\gI^{\otimes(n-k-1)}\otimes\gU{}\otimes \gI^{\otimes{k}}\big)\psi.
\end{equation}
This expression represents multiplying a $2^n\times 2^n$ matrix with a $2^n\times 1$ vector. As the matrix is highly sparse, we can equivalently perform $2^{n-1}$ matrix-vector multiplications in sequential, each time between $\gU{}$ and two of the amplitudes of $\psi$ to improve the efficiency. That is,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:330}
    \gU{}\mat[b]{\psi_{\alpha(t)}\\\psi_{\beta(t)}},\quad t=0,\cdots,2^{n-1}-1.
\end{equation}
%The sequential method reduces the time complexity to $\mathcal O(2^n)$.

More generally, if $\gU$ is a $k$-qubit gate represented by a $2^k\times 2^k$ unitary matrix, then applying $\gU$ on an $n$-qubit statevector $\psi$ consists of $2^{n-k}$ matrix-vector multiplications
\begin{equation}
    \gU\mat[b]{\psi_{\alpha_0}(t)\\\vdots\\\psi_{\alpha_{2^k-1}(t)}},\quad t = 0,\cdots,2^{n-k}-1.
\end{equation}
% The above expression consists of $2^{n-k}$ matrix-vector multiplications, each time between a $2^k\times 2^k$ vector and a length-$2^k$ vector. The time complexity is $\mathcal O(k^22^n)$ and the space complexity is $\mathcal O(2^n+k^4)$. With small $k$, this is asymptotically $\mathcal O(2^n)$ in both time and space. 


